+- Do not ship while the app behavior, onboarding copy, upgrade flow, README/site copy, and settings/menu labels disagree about what Basic includes vs what Pro unlocks.
+
+Required checks:
+1. Define the real Basic vs Pro matrix from code, not memory.
+   - Review actual gates in feature enums, menu actions, settings rows, shortcuts, context menus, and runtime handlers.
+   - Confirm table-stakes features are not accidentally Pro-gated.
+   - Confirm premium features are not leaking through free paths.
+2. Compare every customer-facing explanation against the real gates.
+   - onboarding / welcome flow
+   - upgrade / upsell modals
+   - menu labels and locked-row labels
+   - settings sections
+   - README / website pricing and comparison tables
+3. If a feature is premium, make that visible before the user clicks into it.
+   - Prefer explicit locked labels like `Feature Pro 🔒` or the shared Pro row treatment.
+   - Do not rely on a surprise error or post-click denial as the first signal.
+4. If a feature is described as free in marketing, Basic mode must actually work for that path.
+5. Add or update source tests for the matrix when possible.
+   - Example targets: onboarding lists, locked menu labels, settings gating, permission copy, pricing/comparison copy.
+6. Use calm comparative product language across customer-facing surfaces.
+   - Prefer `Basic includes...` and `Pro includes...`
+   - Guide the user toward Pro by showing the better workflow and differentiators
+   - Do not use awkward internal framing like `Pro First` or blunt `paid version` wording
